The fifth computing block is installed after `@computing-memory-review`. Nine new lessons and one preserved workflow Record form this exact continuation: `computing-durability`, `computing-file-meaning`, `computing-storage-contract`, `computing-crash-states`, `computing-copy-publish`, `computing-recovery-retry`, `rc-atomic`, `computing-storage-cost`, `computing-durable-study`, `computing-durable-review`. Computing now has **51 common learning Records**. The shared workflow keeps its original identity, imports, prose, source assignments and reproducible-computation continuation; only a bounded reading note and common edge were appended. Already selected files/input/output, finite-word, memory-range, process and cost lessons are contextual reuse. All earlier programs and investigations remain unchanged.

The block distinguishes values, encoded bytes, names, schema, volatile caches and durable state. It separates acceptance, atomicity, persistence ordering and durability, and names the failure boundary before claiming survival. The original model assumes one writer, atomic eight-bit word persistence, no surviving old writes after restart, reliable barriers and no corruption of already durable words. Concurrent readers/writers, real device atomic units, filesystem metadata contracts, media failure and external effects are not silently covered. The local record schema is necessary validation, not a proof of transaction history.

The nine-word layout has one selector and two four-word records (sequence,last amount,left stock,right stock). Total stock T≤255 is independently retained configuration. A new request uses the next positive sequence and a positive available transfer; it constructs (seq,amount,left−amount,right+amount). A matching latest positive request is a no-write duplicate, while stale/gapped/conflicting requests are rejected. Sequence 255 can be retried but 256 is rejected rather than wrapping. The protocol writes the inactive record, waits for all candidate words to persist, writes the atomic selector, waits for selector persistence and only then acknowledges.

The active-slot invariant proves old-or-new recovery. Before selector persistence, the old slot remains intact; afterward the first barrier guarantees that the selected candidate is complete. The second barrier proves acknowledged-implies-new. Absence of acknowledgment does not imply absence of effect. Recovery reads/validates without writes; reset and recovery are idempotent. Retrying after recovery either reconstructs the same next record or recognizes the already committed identity. Safe slot reuse assumes no old asynchronous operation or reader survives. Eventual completion additionally assumes crashes stop and pending writes eventually persist; safety alone does not prove liveness.

The original worked case starts with (0,0,12,8) in both slots and selector 0. Request (1,3) produces (1,3,9,11). There are **36 reachable execution states** and **17 distinct durable crash images**: sixteen possible candidate-field subsets with the old selector, plus the complete candidate selected. Every image recovers exactly old or new. A separately written subset oracle constructs the complete expected durable-image set without using the transition function. Direct overwrite can break conservation or publish false request metadata; a schema-valid record can still violate the allowed old/new history. The intentionally broken in-place, missing-data-barrier and missing-selector-barrier variants produce respectively **25,14,1 violating states** in this worked case. These are finite state counts, not failure probabilities.

Retries are explored from every crash image, including crashes during retry. Normal completion after retry applies the transfer once. Later requests (2,2) and (3,1) give stocks (7,13) and (6,14) while alternating slots. Stale requests are rejected. The inactive copy is overwritten and shares the same failure domain, so two slots are not an independent backup or indefinite retained history. A persistence-only additive cost model counts five writes and two barriers per new update, zero writes for a recognized duplicate, and explicitly excludes read/validation/reply costs. With invented w=1,b=10, cost is 5w+2b=25 units, not measured time or device traffic.

Original embedded `durable_study.py` and `durable_checks.py` passed **171,775 checks** through Nix/Python 3.14.8. The **84 first-update cases** cover **3,024 reachable states** and **1,428 case-counted crash images**, with exact independent image-set comparison, all-state recovery/acknowledgment checks, crash-during-retry exploration and **34 slot-reuse cases**. The suite also checks the worked values, malformed representations, request errors, sequence exhaustion and counterexamples for the three defective protocols. Installed programs were extracted into a separate directory and passed the same suite. Integer tuples model storage; neither program performs filesystem crash injection. Finite checks support the explicit invariant/induction proofs without claiming arbitrary hardware correctness or universal exactly-once external effects.

